<p>*Replaced with crit</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Here's some crit:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>can summon any object upon request</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd like some further explanation on how this works. Does she open up the alternate dimension, reach in, and pull out an item? Or is she like Mr. Deeds from <a href="/scp-662">SCP-662</a> and goes out of view, only to return with the item?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>diary</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Diary storytelling is a bit overdone, it's perfectly fine, it's just been done many, many times. Perhaps everything is told through a mix of journal entries and interviews with SCP-XXXX-A?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>discovers her powers on accident</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What was this accident? You don't need to have every single detail in here, but fill in these blanks.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the extent and limitations of her anomalous abilities</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Which are?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>trying to explore the limit of the alternate dimension</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What happens when she does? I just need an overview of what you plan, in addition to what the plan is based off of.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Genre (Optional): Joke</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't seem like it is a -J? I think you should remove this from the genre to avoid confusion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Genre (Optional): Horror</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not really getting any horror vibes from this, a woman can summon objects in her house.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>objects not belonging to direct family members [&#8230;] vanish after a couple hours</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd like to know more about how her and the house's powers work, can she summon anything? Or can she only summon objects belonging to direct family members (those objects then disappearing from wherever they were before)? And what defines ownership of an item that won't disappear?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>XXXX-B summons human leg bones covered in old, decayed flesh, as well as an unknown fluid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Before this point there was no mention of SCP-XXXX-B having the ability to summon anything, and what does this line mean?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Description of SCP told from perspective of a burglar</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The description is written from the perspective of a Foundation researcher trying to convey exactly what the SCP is and does in an effective manner. This does not change unless your SCP has some anomalous property that makes it so (see: <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-2521">●●|●●●●●|●●|●</a>)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>being introduced to the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I like the idea of this, but what exactly happens? What are her thoughts on interacting with the Foundation? If that is indeed what you intend to convey. I need an overview on the execution of the story as well as the story.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

