			<div class="content" id="post-content-4830698">
									

<p>I'll address these in order.<br />
I swapped the order of the entities - XXXX-A is the woman, and XXXX-B is the house. Sorry for confusion.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1) Currently, diary entries describe how she summons things. She first figures out she has some anomalous properties after an interaction with a strange man who enters her house. When asked to find an object, she has an urge to reach forward and grab for it, upon which her hand enters a location not of this dimension. She can then grab the object and retrieve it. Isn't so much like a portal or some large wormhole that opens, but more similar to if you were to reach your hand into a pool of water, and the surface of the water was actually just in the air. (It isn't water, but its the same idea.)</p>
<p>2) I agree with you on the overused diary entries. I'll work on mixing them up with diary and interviews, but I don't want to bog down the article with multiple (possibly 4-5) interview collapsibles. I'm not sure if people want to read that.</p>
<p>3) See number 1.</p>
<p>4) Extent: she can summon any object upon request. Limitations: she has to be able to recognize what they are (not something vague, like the color purple), and she has to be inside whichever house is designated as XXXX-B.</p>
<p>5) I'm really not sure what to do here in terms of just XXXX-A exploring the dimension on her own. I want to start to introduce the Foundation more heavily around this point.</p>
<p>6) Will remove both joke and horror. I don't know what to categorize this SCP as, though.</p>
<p>7) See 4. Additionally, what I wish to define as an item that won't disappear is any item that is owned directly by either XXXX-A, her husband, or her children. Things such as furniture, a television, beds, microwave, etc, do not disappear. But if, say, XXXX-A's mother-in-law's cousin visits, and leaves her glasses in the house (XXXX-B), the glasses will disappear after some time.</p>
<p>8) I didn't word this properly. The description is written in a clinical, SCP-Researcher tone. There is a police report inserted in the Description section which contains an interview between a police officer and a burglar who broke into XXXX-A's house (XXXX-B).</p>
<p>9) I haven't gotten to the actual meat and potatoes of her introduction to the foundation yet. The Foundation will eventually obtain a copy of the police report and send a field agent to investigate, upon which the Foundation will respond with a bit more presence (possibly a supposed gas leak, evacuating nearby neighbors, leaving the Foundation free to inspect both the house and SCP-XXXX-A and her family for 'illness' or whatever happens when you're exposed to lots of natural gas.)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thank you for your criticism. I'll make the appropriate changes.</p>

							</div>

