<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>will be told from a first person perspective of XXXX-A</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This line here, and the central narrative section as a whole, raises one question to me: why isn't this a tale? Like it absolutely could work as a SCP! But the focus here feels very much on the woman and what she wants and how she feels about everything, and to me, that's often best written as a tale where you can really like&#8230;just get in her head in a way you can't with diary entries and interviews.</p>
<p>Beyond this, my biggest concern is you have this really nice setting for a story, where you have all these elements and ways to get them into play, but you don't really do much with them: once she's in containment, not much really happens and it feels like this is just&#8230;really calling out for more conflict and the story to like&#8230; go somewhere, y'know?</p>
<p>Like, you mention:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>in return, she performs tests with the Foundation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But it's not clear either what these tests are or how they're tying into the narrative here and at the end of the day, this feels&#8230;almost like a slice of life piece but where there's only really one character and I'm not sure there's enough meat here to get away with it?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

